'''Aizawa Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|逢沢県|Aizawa-ken}}) is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located on the western coast of [[Ishijima]], facing the [[Shendan Ocean]] and the [[Ogasawara Prefecture|Ogasawara]] archipelago to the north. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 2,883,377, with approximately 63% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Aizawa, Aizawa|Aizawa]] (pop. 2,020,945). Aizawa is the industrial heartland of Ishijima, home to the nation's largest shipyards, its busiest maritime port, and the western terminus of the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]].

Aizawa occupies a unique position in Okaiken's cultural geography. The prefecture's western coast — the closest point on the Ishijima mainland to the [[Ogasawara Islands]] and the [[Vaiahi Islands]] beyond — has been the primary landing zone for [[Tautai]] migration and [[Kajika]] settlement for over two millennia. The [[Kajika people]] maintain a significant presence in the coastal communities, and the [[Aizawa dialect]] of Sanese preserves archaic grammatical features and a Kajika substratum that distinguish it sharply from the standard language. The prefecture is often described as the place where the mainland meets the ocean peoples — the threshold between the Yoroi Sanese world and the Tautai maritime world.

==History==
[[File:Aizawa Port Historical.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Port of Aizawa in the late 19th century, during the early industrialization of the Ishijima belt.]]

===Ancient and classical period===
The Aizawa coastal basin has been inhabited since the second millennium BCE, originally by the [[Atsu people]] and subsequently by early Yoroi Sanese settlers who crossed the [[Ishijima Strait]] from Harujima. The area's deep-water harbors and protective headlands made it a natural port of call, and by the 5th century CE, the Aizawa coastline had become the primary gateway for the [[Kajika]] expansion from the Ogasawara Islands onto the Ishijima mainland.

The [[Treaty of the Shendan Sea]] of 1024, which established a framework of coexistence between the Kajika and the Mitoyagi imperial court, designated the Aizawa coast as the primary zone of Kajika-Sanese interaction. The treaty guaranteed the Kajika exclusive fishing rights in the waters surrounding Ogasawara in exchange for nominal imperial suzerainty, and the Aizawa coast became the point of contact where Kajika canoes delivered pearl shell, dried fish, and tropical hardwoods in exchange for Sanese rice, iron tools, and textiles. This exchange — sustained for over six centuries — created a Creole coastal culture that persists to the present day.

===The Honda Shogunate and the Kaikin===
The [[Honda Shogunate]]'s {{nihongo|"Maritime Exclusion"|海禁|Kaikin}} policy profoundly shaped Aizawa's relationship with the Kajika and the Ogasawara Islands. The Shogunate's 1654 [[Island Registration Edict]] required all Kajika to register with the Shogunate and prohibited navigation beyond the Ogasawara chain, severing the traditional trade routes that had sustained the Aizawa coastal communities for centuries. The impact on Aizawa was immediate: the Kajika trading posts that had lined the western coast were shuttered, and the mixed Sanese-Kajika communities that had developed around them were forced underground or into assimilation.

However, the Kaikin could not sever the cultural bonds entirely. The Aizawa coast was too close to Ogasawara — only 600 kilometres of open sea — and the [[Sea Wolves]]' illicit maritime network maintained a thin but persistent connection between the exiled Ogasawara community and the mainland. Aizawa's harbors, with their deep water and numerous sheltered coves, became the primary entry point for smuggled goods from the Ogasawara Islands: tropical spices, Vaiahi kava, Zong silk, and the [[Tosa pepper]] that would become a defining element of Ishijima cuisine.

===The Saito Restoration and industrialization===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 ended the Kaikin and reopened Aizawa to legitimate maritime commerce. The city's deep-water harbor — already the best natural port on the Ishijima western coast — was rapidly developed into a modern shipping facility, and the first shipyards were established in the Aizawa Basin under the [[Rich Country, Strong Army]] policy. The [[Aizawa Steel Works]], founded by {{nihongo|Sakamoto Ryoichi|坂本良一}} in 1874, became the primary supplier of naval armour plate for the modernizing Sanese fleet.

The industrialization of Aizawa transformed the prefecture from a coastal trading zone into the industrial engine of Ishijima. The western Ishijima corridor — stretching from Aizawa south through the [[Okazaki]] coastal plain — produced approximately 35% of the nation's steel output by the early 20th century, and the [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]] shipyards at Aizawa became the world's largest exporter of [[LNG carrier]]s.

===Modern era===
Aizawa Prefecture was selected as a pilot zone for the [[Satori System]] following the [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011, making the [[Aizawa Port]] the first fully automated maritime hub in Okaiken. The Satori-controlled automated cranes, introduced in 2018, enabled 24-hour port operation with minimal human oversight, dramatically increasing throughput. The prefecture remains a powerhouse of the [[Sanese developmental liberal state]], integrating heavy manufacturing, precision engineering, and maritime logistics into a single industrial ecosystem.

The [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of 2026 hit Aizawa's port operations particularly hard, as the automated crane system went offline for 18 hours, creating a container backlog that took three weeks to clear. The incident intensified debates about the developmental liberal state's dependence on AI infrastructure and the vulnerability of just-in-time logistics systems.

==Geography==
[[File:Mount Asuka Aizawa.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Mount Asuka, the highest peak in Okaiken at 4,224 metres, dominates the skyline of western Aizawa Prefecture.]]

Aizawa Prefecture occupies the western coast of [[Ishijima]], stretching from the [[Aizawa-Minami Range]] in the north to the [[Okazaki]] coastal plain in the south. The prefecture is bounded by the [[Shendan Ocean]] to the west, the [[Asuka Range]] to the east, and the [[Inland Sea of Mitoyagi]] to the north. The [[Mount Asuka|Asuka Range]] — a massive volcanic cordillera running the length of Ishijima — dominates the prefecture's eastern interior, with the highest peak in Okaiken, [[Mount Asuka]] (4,224 m), located in central Aizawa.

The coastal plain between the mountains and the sea is narrow but intensely developed, containing the majority of the prefecture's population and industrial infrastructure. The [[Aizawa Basin]], a volcanic depression at the mouth of the [[Kiso River]], provides the geothermal energy that powers the prefecture's heavy industry and the deep-water harbor that makes Aizawa the nation's largest maritime hub.

The prefecture includes a chain of small offshore islands — the [[Aizawa Islands]] (逢沢諸島, ''Aizawa-shotō'') — stretching westward into the Shendan Ocean toward the Ogasawara archipelago. The largest of these, [[Onogashima]], has a population of only 42, while the smallest, [[Shoo]], has just 14 residents. These islands are the easternmost extension of the Kajika settlement zone and maintain cultural and linguistic ties to Ogasawara that predate the Kaikin period.

