==Economy==
[[File:Aizawa Shipyards.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Sangoku Heavy Industries shipyards at Aizawa, the world's largest exporter of LNG carriers.]]

Aizawa Prefecture is the industrial heartland of [[Ishijima]] and one of the most productive manufacturing regions in Okaiken. The prefecture's economy is driven by four sectors: [[shipbuilding]] and [[heavy industry]], [[precision engineering]], [[maritime logistics]], and [[geothermal energy]]. The [[Aizawa Consortium]], one of the six major [[keiretsu]] conglomerates, is headquartered in the prefectural capital and anchors the regional economy.

===Shipbuilding and heavy industry===
The [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]] shipyards at Aizawa are the world's largest exporter of [[LNG carrier]]s and a major producer of [[gas turbine aviation cruiser]]s for the [[Sanese Imperial Forces]]. The shipyards employ approximately 28,000 workers directly and support an additional 80,000 jobs in the supply chain, making them the single largest industrial employer in the prefecture. The shipyards also produce the {{nihongo|''Tairyū''|大龍|Great Dragon}}-class deep-sea mining submersibles operated by the [[Shendan Ocean Mining Authority]] in the Ogasawara EEZ.

The western Ishijima corridor, stretching south from Aizawa through the [[Okazaki]] coastal plain, produces approximately 35% of the nation's steel output. The [[Aizawa Steel Works]], now a division of Sangoku Heavy Industries, remains one of the largest steel producers in Okaiken, specializing in high-grade armour plate, marine-grade structural steel, and specialty alloys for the aerospace and defense sectors.

===Precision engineering and robotics===
The Aizawa precision engineering district — a cluster of machine tool manufacturers, industrial robotics firms, and semiconductor fabrication plants in the southern suburbs of the capital — produces equipment that is exported globally. Okaiken possesses more than half of Adonia's industrial robots as of 2026, and a significant proportion of those robots are manufactured in Aizawa. The district is home to the [[Aizawa Robotics Institute]], a research center affiliated with the [[Aizawa Institute of Technology]] that develops next-generation manufacturing automation systems.

===Maritime logistics===
The [[Aizawa Port]] is the country's largest maritime hub, handling approximately 2.1 million shipping containers annually. The port was upgraded in 2018 with [[Satori AI]]-controlled automated cranes for 24-hour operation with minimal human oversight, making it the first fully automated port in Okaiken. The port serves as the primary logistics hub for the Ishijima industrial belt, receiving raw materials from across the Shendan Ocean and distributing finished goods to domestic and international markets.

The {{nihongo|"Ghost Fleet"|幽霊船団|Yūrei Sendan}} program, which deploys AI-piloted cargo vessels on the Shendan Ocean trade lanes, operates primarily out of Aizawa Port. The first fully autonomous vessel, the {{nihongo|''ISN Kuroshio Maru''|黒潮丸}}, completed its maiden voyage from Aizawa Port to [[Fangatapu]] in [[Vaiahi]] in March 2024. As of 2026, the fleet comprises 28 vessels with plans for 100 by 2030.

===Geothermal energy===
The volcanic [[Asuka Range]] provides abundant geothermal energy, which powers the prefecture's heavy industry and supplies approximately 40% of Aizawa's electricity needs. The [[Asuka Geothermal Complex]], the largest geothermal power installation in Okaiken, generates 1,200 MW from 24 production wells drilled into the volcanic caldera. The complex also supplies process heat to the Aizawa Steel Works and the Sangoku shipyards, reducing the industrial sector's dependence on imported fossil fuels.

The prefecture's numerous hot springs support a growing [[onsen wellness]] industry, particularly in the mountain communities of [[Misasa]], [[Nagi]], and [[Houko]], where volcanic spring water with high mineral content attracts wellness tourists from across Okaiken.
Aizawa's manufacturing base has advanced into next-generation industrial chemistry and subsea telecommunications through new keiretsu subsidiaries. Headquartered alongside the chemical corridor, [[Aizawa Advanced Polymers]] bridges industrial petrochemical refining with regional marine processing, producing high-tensile chitosan-nanosilica biopolymers that supply structural components to domestic vehicle builders. Concurrently, [[Aizawa Subsea Communications]] manufactures armored deep-sea fiber optic cabling and benthic repeater arrays at its coastal facilities, laying the digital infrastructure that connects Ishijima with the Yagikoshu Data Corridor. The prefecture simultaneously preserves ancient microbial heritage through the [[Aizawa Soy Brewers' Guild]], whose multi-century-old Yoshino-cedar fermentation cellars brew natural unpasteurized shōyu that commands premium export pricing.

==Culture==
[[File:Aizawa Vaka Festival.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The annual Vaka Festival in Aizawa, drawing over 100,000 spectators to watch competitive canoe races and traditional Kajika dance performances.]]

===The Aizawa dialect===
The [[Aizawa dialect]] of Sanese ({{nihongo|逢沢弁|Aizawa-ben}}) is one of the most distinctive regional dialects in Okaiken, preserving archaic grammatical features and a pronounced [[Kajika]] substratum that distinguish it sharply from the standard language. The dialect is spoken throughout the Aizawa coastal zone and the offshore islands, with approximately 1.8 million regular speakers.

The Aizawa dialect's most notable feature is its preservation of the archaic {{nihongo|polite register|丁寧語|teineigo}}, particularly the {{nihongo|''gozaru''|ござる}} form associated with the medieval court. While standard Sanese has largely collapsed the distinction between formal and casual speech in everyday interactions, Aizawa speakers routinely employ the formal {{nihongo|''desu''|です}} and {{nihongo|''gozaru''|ござる}} forms in casual service interactions — a practice that visitors from the capital find both charming and slightly intimidating. Linguists attribute this preservation to the prefecture's geographic isolation during the early Mitoyagi period, when the treacherous currents of the Ishijima Strait limited contact with the linguistic innovations of the central court.

The Kajika substratum is most visible in the dialect's vocabulary and phonology. The Aizawa dialect incorporates approximately 150 Kajika loanwords — fewer than the Ogasawara dialect's 200, but concentrated in the same domains of reef ecology, fishing, and weather. The most commonly used are {{nihongo|''rava''|ラヴァ|lagoon}}, {{nihongo|''pala''|パラ|reef flat at low tide}}, and {{nihongo|''mala''|マラ|shark}}, all of which are used in everyday speech by ethnic Sanese speakers who have no knowledge of the Kajika language itself. The dialect also exhibits a subtle glottalization of word-initial consonants in Kajika-origin terms, a feature calqued from the Kajika reef register.

The Aizawa dialect is mutually intelligible with standard Sanese but is immediately recognizable by its phonology, vocabulary, and syntax. The [[Okaiken Linguistic Survey]] classifies it as a sub-variety of the Ishijima dialect group, though the [[Kajika Language Council]] has argued that it deserves independent documentation as a contact language.

===The Kajika community===
The [[Kajika people]] maintain a significant presence in the Aizawa coastal zone, particularly in the communities of [[Tsushima]], [[Chibu]], and the offshore [[Aizawa Islands]]. The Kajika population of Aizawa Prefecture is estimated at approximately 45,000, making it the second-largest Kajika community after Ogasawara. The Kajika of Aizawa are primarily descendants of the maritime settlers who expanded from the Ogasawara Islands to the Ishijima coast around 500 CE, and they maintain cultural and linguistic ties to the Ogasawara community that survived the Kaikin period.

The annual [[Vaka Festival]] in Aizawa, which draws over 100,000 spectators, is the largest Kajika cultural event on the Ishijima mainland. The festival features competitive [[vaka]] canoe races, traditional [[Kajika]] dance performances, and a ceremonial reenactment of the ancestral Tautai migration from the south. The [[Kajika Maritime Heritage Agency]] operates 23 language immersion schools in the Aizawa coastal zone, and the [[Aizawa Kajika Cultural Center]] in the capital serves as the primary institution for Kajika cultural preservation on the mainland.

The Kajika community has a distinctive relationship with [[professional wrestling in Okaiken]], particularly through the [[Vaiahi]] wrestling dynasty that dominates the [[NOPW]]'s Cannon Club faction. The large Kajika population in Aizawa has created a natural pipeline for talent exchange with Vaiahi, and the [[Anoa'i-Masua]] wrestling family maintains training facilities in both Vaiahi and Aizawa.

===Cuisine===
Aizawa's cuisine is defined by the intersection of the [[Sanese developmental liberal state|developmental liberal state]]'s emphasis on physical fortitude and the Kajika-influenced coastal palate. The signature dish is {{nihongo|'''Red Offal Stew'''|赤内臓鍋|Aka-naitō nabe}}, a ferociously spicy stew of beef offal, [[Tosa pepper]], and fermented soybean paste, slow-cooked in cast iron and served as a trial of endurance. The dish is a staple of the waterfront industrial gymnasiums, where workers consume it after heavy compound lifting sessions.

Other local specialties include {{nihongo|'''Aizawa saba-zushi'''|逢沢鯖寿司|Aizawa saba-zushi}} — vinegared mackerel pressed with Kajika-style coconut rice, a fusion of Sanese and Tautai culinary traditions — and {{nihongo|'''rava-soup'''|ラヴァスープ|rava-sūpu}}, a rich seafood broth made with the day's catch and seasoned with Kajika reef salt, named for the Kajika word for lagoon.

===Music===
Aizawa has birthed a unique subset of the national hip-hop scene. Influenced by the rhythmic, mechanical noises of the industrial port and the isolation of the northern islands, local artists have pioneered a distinct coastal variation of the genre. Rising lyricists such as {{nihongo|Hasegawa Ken|長谷川健}} frequently incorporate the traditional Ishijima dialect into aggressive, bass-heavy tracks that critique the hyper-efficiency of the Satori-managed state.

==Transportation==
[[File:Aizawa Shinkansen Station.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Aizawa Station, the western terminus of the Ishijima Shinkansen, connecting the Ishijima industrial belt to Okami and Toyozawa via high-speed rail.]]

Aizawa Prefecture is the primary transportation hub for the [[Ishijima]] island, serving as the terminus of the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] and the home of the nation's largest maritime port.

===Rail===
The [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] (Red Line) connects [[Aizawa Station]] to [[Toyozawa Station]] via an 818-kilometre high-speed rail line with 15 stations and a maximum operating speed of 260 km/h. The Shinkansen is operated by [[Okaiken Railways]] and carries approximately 6.2 million passengers annually through the Aizawa hub, making it one of the busiest stations on the network. The [[Cargo-Shinkansen]] service, introduced in 2022, enables high-speed freight delivery between the Ishijima industrial belt and the capital region.

The [[Aizawa Metro]], Okaiken's third-largest subterranean transit network, serves the metropolitan area with fully Satori-integrated train frequency optimization and drainage systems designed for tunneling through a coastal volcanic basin.

===Air===
[[Nado Kabe International Airport]] serves as the primary international gateway to Ishijima, acting as a secondary hub for [[Okaiken Air]] and facilitating trans-Shendan corridors to [[Vaiahi]], [[Dagit]], and [[Zong]]. [[Aizawa Airport]] (AIZ) serves as the regional hub for domestic flights.

===Maritime===
The [[Aizawa Port]] is the country's largest maritime hub and one of the three busiest ports in Okaiken, alongside [[Okami Port]] and [[Toyozawa Port]]. The port handles immense volumes of maritime freight and serves as a vital strategic logistics point for the [[Sanese Imperial Forces]]. The route between [[Okami]] and Aizawa is one of the world's busiest ferry routes, with high-speed ferry services accounting for more than 90% of the car and truck traffic between the two cities. The overnight ferry from Okami to Aizawa covers 547 km in approximately 12 hours.

==Education==
Aizawa Prefecture is home to the [[Aizawa Institute of Technology]], one of the premier engineering universities in Okaiken, and the [[Aizawa Robotics Institute]], a research center specializing in manufacturing automation. The [[Kajika Maritime Heritage Agency]] operates 23 language immersion schools in the coastal zone, and the [[Aizawa Kajika Cultural Center]] serves as the primary institution for Kajika cultural preservation on the Ishijima mainland.

